.TH join "" "Command"
.SH NAME
join \- join two databases
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBjoin [\-a[\fIn\fB]] [\-e \fIs\fB] [\-j[\fIn\fB] \fIkey\fB] [\-o \fIn.m\fB ...] [\-t\fIc\fB] \fIfile1 file2\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
join -- join two databases
Usage:	join [ options ] file1 file2
Options:
	-a[n]	Print unpaired records from file 'n'
	-e s	Replace empty fields on output with string 's'
	-j[n] key
		Use field 'key' of file 'n' for comparison
	-o [n.]m ..
		Subsequent arguments constitute a list of which fields to
		output;  each element has file 'n' and a field number 'm'
	-tc	The field separator (input and output) is the character 'c'
If either of 'file1' or 'file2' is '-', the standard input is used.
The optional file number 'n' may be either '1' or '2';
if omitted, both '1' and '2' are assumed.
.HE
.B join
reads
.I file1
and
.IR file2 ,
each a text file holding one record per line, and writes to the
standard output a line for every pair of records, one from each
file, whose
.I key
fields compare equal, followed (unless
.B \-a
is given) by nothing else.
Either file name may be
.BR \- ,
meaning the standard input; both may not be
.B \-
at once.
.PP
Each input file must already be sorted on its key field, for example
with
.BR sort ,
using the same field and collating order as
.BR join .
By default fields are separated by runs of blanks and tabs, which
are also stripped from the front of each field; with
.B \-t
every occurrence of the single separator character delimits a
field, so empty fields are possible.
By default the key field of each file is field 1.
.SH Options
.IP "\fB\-a\fR[\fIn\fB]" 0.6i
Besides the normal joined output, also print records of file
.I n
whose key did not match any record of the other file.
If
.I n
is omitted, do this for both files.
.IP "\fB\-e \fIs\fR"
On output, print
.I s
in place of any field that is empty.
.IP "\fB\-j\fR[\fIn\fB] \fIkey\fR"
Use field number
.I key
(counting from 1), instead of field 1, as the join field of file
.IR n ;
if
.I n
is omitted, set the join field of both files.
.IP "\fB\-o \fIn.m\fR ..."
Print only the listed fields, in the order given, instead of the
default output; each
.I n.m
selects field
.I m
of file
.IR n .
This option consumes all the remaining
.I n.m
arguments up to the two file name arguments.
.IP \fB\-t\fIc\fR
Use the single character
.I c
as the field separator on both input and output, instead of runs of
blanks and tabs.
.PP
With no
.B \-o
option, the default output for a matched pair is the key field,
followed by the remaining fields of the
.I file1
record, followed by the remaining fields of the
.I file2
record.
Fields on output are separated by
.I c
if
.B \-t
was given, otherwise by a single space.
.SH Example
.DS
join \-t: \-j1 3 \-o 1.3 2.4 1.4 1.1 2.2 filea fileb
.DE
joins
.B filea
and
.B fileb
on fields separated by
.BR : ,
using field 3 as the join field of
.B filea
and field 1 (the default) of
.BR fileb ,
printing the five listed fields of each matched pair.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR awk ,
.BR comm ,
.BR sort ,
.B uniq
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B join
prints
.B "join field is missing in input"
if a record has fewer fields than the join field requires, and exits
nonzero if either input file cannot be opened, if both are
.BR \- ,
or if the command line is malformed.
